WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:06.980
So the last time we tried to film chlorine
experiments we

00:00:06.980 --> 00:00:10.580
found that the chlorine cylinder itself had
a slight

00:00:10.580 --> 00:00:16.910
problem, it was seized here. But the Stig
has acquired

00:00:16.910 --> 00:00:20.750
another cylinder of chlorine. So you know
what this

00:00:20.750 --> 00:00:26.100
means, it means that we feel duty-bound to
increase the

00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:29.050
intensity of our chlorine video and show you
some

00:00:29.050 --> 00:00:34.670
chemistry with chlorine.

00:00:34.670 --> 00:00:38.450
Chlorine is an element that many people have
heard of.

00:00:38.450 --> 00:00:44.530
It’s a greenish-yellow gas which consists
of, the gas

00:00:44.530 --> 00:00:48.170
consists of two chlorine atoms bonded together
so it’s

00:00:48.170 --> 00:00:51.710
Cl2 and it’s much heavier than air.

00:00:51.710 --> 00:00:56.120
Incredibly reactive, incredibly poisonous,
not a very nice

00:00:56.120 --> 00:01:00.120
compound at all. Ok so Neil is about to open
the cylinder

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:02.989
of chlorine to allow some of the chlorine
gas to leak

00:01:02.989 --> 00:01:08.490
through the pipe into the, to the flask in
the fume hood.

00:01:08.490 --> 00:01:10.500
Chlorine sits in the right-hand side of the
periodic table

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:13.960
in the halogens in group, well, some people
say seven,

00:01:13.960 --> 00:01:18.290
some people say seventeen. Really what that
says is

00:01:18.290 --> 00:01:21.380
that chlorine wants another electron to get
that noble

00:01:21.380 --> 00:01:25.260
gas configuration and it will move heaven
and earth to

00:01:25.260 --> 00:01:26.260
get that electron!

00:01:26.260 --> 00:01:32.369
So during the First World War it was used
as a chemical

00:01:32.369 --> 00:01:39.920
weapon. The gas could spread across the battlefields
and when it came to the trenches, which were

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:43.250
holes in
the ground, it would fall into the trenches

00:01:43.250 --> 00:01:45.399
and fill them
up.

00:01:45.399 --> 00:01:47.509
You can see the yellow colour against the
white

00:01:47.509 --> 00:01:50.650
background on the paper, so now we’re starting
to fill

00:01:50.650 --> 00:01:53.740
that flask with chlorine; it’s quite dense,
so it will stick

00:01:53.740 --> 00:01:56.189
to the bottom of the flask it’s not like
a light gas like

00:01:56.189 --> 00:01:59.659
hydrogen or helium, it won’t come upwards.
So now you

00:01:59.659 --> 00:02:04.000
can see the colour in that chlorine is really
quite intense.

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:07.460
It wasn’t a terribly good weapon because
it could quite

00:02:07.460 --> 00:02:10.920
easily, if the wind changed direction, it
could blow the

00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:14.720
gas back to the people who were letting it
out.

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:17.620
So we’ll just put a stopper in to contain
the gas while we

00:02:17.620 --> 00:02:19.810
prepare the rest of the experiment.

00:02:19.810 --> 00:02:23.670
Chlorine is quite corrosive, it reacts with
water to make

00:02:23.670 --> 00:02:29.099
an acid, HCl, hydrochloric acid which can
attack people’s

00:02:29.099 --> 00:02:35.110
lungs. The lungs generate fluid, they fill
up with fluid and

00:02:35.110 --> 00:02:41.080
quickly the person drowns in their own fluid.
It also

00:02:41.080 --> 00:02:45.560
corrodes things very easily so all the metal
fittings on

00:02:45.560 --> 00:02:50.310
people’s battle dress, the brass buttons,
brass buckles,

00:02:50.310 --> 00:02:54.040
all went green as well. But it’s not a terribly
effective

00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:57.180
weapon but when people weren’t expecting
any sort of

00:02:57.180 --> 00:03:01.959
chemical weapon it must have been terrifying.

00:03:01.959 --> 00:03:05.560
Ok so we have a small amount of aluminium
here which

00:03:05.560 --> 00:03:08.319
we put on the end of a wire. We’re gonna
heat the

00:03:08.319 --> 00:03:11.900
aluminium in that flame and then we’re gonna
put the

00:03:11.900 --> 00:03:14.470
hot aluminium into the flask of chlorine,
then hopefully

00:03:14.470 --> 00:03:17.739
we’ll see, the chlorine will react with
the aluminium and

00:03:17.739 --> 00:03:22.870
we’ll get very rapid formation of aluminium
trichloride,

00:03:22.870 --> 00:03:24.930
AlCl3.

00:03:24.930 --> 00:03:30.840
The British Army created whole regiments of
gas

00:03:30.840 --> 00:03:35.680
soldiers who were all trained chemists, who
were sent

00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:40.490
out to handle these cylinders of gas. And
on the German

00:03:40.490 --> 00:03:45.010
side in the First World War, Fritz Haber,
the inventor of

00:03:45.010 --> 00:03:48.730
the Haber process to make ammonia became very
keen

00:03:48.730 --> 00:04:00.129
on chemical weapons and he was the chief proponent.
His wife was really furious about this and

00:04:00.129 --> 00:04:06.090
in fact shot
herself with his revolver, killed herself,

00:04:06.090 --> 00:04:10.750
the day before
he was due to go back to the front. And he,

00:04:10.750 --> 00:04:15.530
it was said
that this was because she objected to his

00:04:15.530 --> 00:04:20.070
work on
chemical weapons. Though at the same time

00:04:20.070 --> 00:04:22.480
there were
rumours that he was having an affair with

00:04:22.480 --> 00:04:25.040
another
woman, so there may have been other factors

00:04:25.040 --> 00:04:26.690
at play as
well.

00:04:26.690 --> 00:04:34.370
So, let’s heat up our aluminium. Ok, so
it’s nice and

00:04:34.370 --> 00:04:43.150
warm, all over. And then we’ll drop it or
add it into the

00:04:43.150 --> 00:04:50.910
chlorine glass. Now instantly we are seeing
aluminium

00:04:50.910 --> 00:04:52.460
trichloride coming out of the top and reacting
with

00:04:52.460 --> 00:04:55.940
moisture in the air. So now you can see the
reaction is

00:04:55.940 --> 00:04:57.720
getting very intense and we are seeing a flame
in the

00:04:57.720 --> 00:05:01.080
bottom. So the aluminium is being consumed
as it’s

00:05:01.080 --> 00:05:04.810
reacting, oxidising strongly in that chlorine-rich
air.

00:05:04.810 --> 00:05:09.400
Chlorine is found all over the world, the
sea contains

00:05:09.400 --> 00:05:14.400
sodium chloride, and there are huge deposits
of sodium

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:19.330
chloride in salt lakes where lakes have dried
out, such

00:05:19.330 --> 00:05:23.210
as in some parts of the States, where you,
United

00:05:23.210 --> 00:05:27.550
States, where you have salt lakes where people
can

00:05:27.550 --> 00:05:31.880
race cars and things across the flat surface
and in other

00:05:31.880 --> 00:05:33.509
parts of the world.

00:05:33.509 --> 00:05:37.229
All of the aluminium has been consumed now
by the

00:05:37.229 --> 00:05:39.870
chlorine: a very, very rapid reaction.

00:05:39.870 --> 00:05:43.710
There are many areas, such as in the north
of England,

00:05:43.710 --> 00:05:48.660
in the county of Cheshire and also in Salzburg
in Austria,

00:05:48.660 --> 00:05:53.460
where there are prehistoric deposits of salt
from

00:05:53.460 --> 00:05:56.760
prehistoric salt lakes, which can now be mined
really

00:05:56.760 --> 00:06:01.510
quite easily, and there, so salt and chlorine
is a very

00:06:01.510 --> 00:06:10.520
common element. It’s made from sodium chloride
solution by electrolysis, by passing an electric

00:06:10.520 --> 00:06:16.229
current,
and it’s a very energy intensive process.

00:06:16.229 --> 00:06:20.669
One percent of
the UK’s electricity was used, at one time,

00:06:20.669 --> 00:06:25.240
for making
chlorine from sodium chloride, and the chlorine

00:06:25.240 --> 00:06:31.650
is used
particularly for making the plastic PVC.

00:06:31.650 --> 00:06:35.080
While we got chlorine here, we thought we’d
do a couple

00:06:35.080 --> 00:06:37.880
of reactions. So we’ve got some chlorine
in this flask

00:06:37.880 --> 00:06:40.850
and you can see the nice yellow colour and
I thought

00:06:40.850 --> 00:06:42.830
what we’d try and do now is do a competition
reaction

00:06:42.830 --> 00:06:46.940
again or a reaction with iron. So now the
iron, we’re

00:06:46.940 --> 00:06:49.240
gonna heat the iron, this is iron wool which
you might

00:06:49.240 --> 00:06:52.190
use for treating wood, and we’re gonna heat
that until

00:06:52.190 --> 00:06:54.680
it’s warm and put it in the chlorine, the
chlorine will

00:06:54.680 --> 00:06:57.150
oxidise it to iron chloride.

00:06:57.150 --> 00:07:01.289
Chlorine will react with all sorts of metals.
You can make

00:07:01.289 --> 00:07:06.990
salt by burning the metal sodium in chlorine,
and if you

00:07:06.990 --> 00:07:11.430
put in other metals like aluminium or iron
they will also

00:07:11.430 --> 00:07:16.280
react. Aluminium reacts to form aluminium
trichloride

00:07:16.280 --> 00:07:19.120
which is the salt of aluminium and iron will
similarly

00:07:19.120 --> 00:07:24.449
react to form iron chloride, and most metals
but not all

00:07:24.449 --> 00:07:27.320
of them will react with chlorine: lead will
not for

00:07:27.320 --> 00:07:34.130
example. But it is really quite a reactive
gas.

00:07:34.130 --> 00:07:37.740
So really rapid reaction there as the hot
iron starts to

00:07:37.740 --> 00:07:41.530
react with the chlorine to form iron chloride.
Very, very

00:07:41.530 --> 00:07:44.190
exothermic again and I think, yes this is
very hot, we

00:07:44.190 --> 00:07:45.849
might have to move it off the paper.

00:07:45.849 --> 00:07:50.630
So what happens in all of these reactions
is that the

00:07:50.630 --> 00:07:55.379
chlorine removes an electron from the metal
to make

00:07:55.379 --> 00:08:01.360
the chloride ion Cl-, and the chloride ion
is perfectly

00:08:01.360 --> 00:08:06.740
innocuous, you can drink, you can eat chloride,
whereas

00:08:06.740 --> 00:08:09.090
chlorine itself is very poisonous.

00:08:09.090 --> 00:08:13.419
Oh it’s warm, it’s not too hot to touch
its like picking up

00:08:13.419 --> 00:08:16.940
a mug of warm tea or warm coffee. But you
can see

00:08:16.940 --> 00:08:20.650
again the products of the chemical reaction
have

00:08:20.650 --> 00:08:23.700
precipitated out across the flask, and now
if I remove

00:08:23.700 --> 00:08:28.699
the…wire again I can see that all of the
iron has been

00:08:28.699 --> 00:08:30.450
consumed again instantly. The moment that
it got into

00:08:30.450 --> 00:08:34.240
that chlorine-rich flask; very, very rapid
reaction, very,

00:08:34.240 --> 00:08:35.439
very reactive with chlorine.

